timo_dallo_Stagnone_di_Marsala.JPG/500px-Vista_di_marettimo_dallo_Stagnone_di_Marsala.JPG","caption":"Stagnone Lagoon","author":"Peppino091163","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_di_marettimo_dallo_Stagnone_di_Marsala.JPG","w":3264,"h":2448,"ok_print":true}],"intro":{"body":"Trapani occupies a narrow peninsula stretching between two seas, where the Tyrrhenian and Mediterranean meet. Its historic centre extends directly into the water, framed by old city walls, beaches, and a working port. As the capital of the surrounding province, Trapani has long balanced maritime trade with civic and administrative importance. Fishing boats, ferries, and cruise ships still shape daily life, while the seafront promenade along the Tramontana walls offers some of the city’s most distinctive views. For many years Trapani remained largely outside Sicily’s main tourist circuits, but this is gradually changing.","grounding":[{"source":"wikivoyage","title":"Trapani","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Trapani","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"short":"Trapani is a coastal city in Italy. Trapani Cathedral and Monte Erice make up most of nine hours of sightseeing, and Playa San Vito Lo Capo lies close by for the beach, though public transport is limited, so May to July is the safer window.","short_src":"rewrite","facts":{"population":67531,"visit_h":6.9}},"highlights":[{"id":"hl-trapani-cathedral","name":"Trapani Cathedral","kind":"Cathedral","lat":38.01612,"lon":12.50807,"dist_km":2.5,"rank_score":8.35,"resolved_by":"catalogue","image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Cattedraletrapaniesterno.JPG/960px-Cattedraletrapaniesterno.JPG","author":"Lelezaff at Italian Wikipedia","licence":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cattedraletrapaniesterno.JPG","w":1024,"h":768,"ok_print":true}},{"id":"hl-eryx","name":"Eryx","kind":"Ruins","l